WEBVTT

00:00:00.117 --> 00:00:02.834
Look at a pay stub. Or
a property tax bill,

00:00:03.174 --> 00:00:05.194
or a receipt with sales tax on it.

00:00:05.441 --> 00:00:09.174
There is a line on it where your
money leaves you and becomes part

00:00:09.174 --> 00:00:10.874
of somebody else's budget.

00:00:11.463 --> 00:00:14.483
Almost nobody ever
finds what happens next.

00:00:14.743 --> 00:00:18.123
Not because it's a secret, but
because the answer is long and

00:00:18.123 --> 00:00:20.023
dull and written for auditors.

00:00:20.733 --> 00:00:24.913
Your city publishes its budget, but
it's usually a few hundred pages and

00:00:24.913 --> 00:00:27.313
it's full of lines that read:

00:00:27.313 --> 00:00:31.533
fund zero-one-zero, department zero-eight,

00:00:31.533 --> 00:00:32.836
account four-two-zero-one-zero.

00:00:33.505 --> 00:00:37.225
Technically you can read it, but
technically it's carrying a lot of weight

00:00:37.225 --> 00:00:41.549
in that sentence. Public and
readable are not the same thing.

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:44.130
This is Treasury Tracker.

00:00:45.252 --> 00:00:49.267
Treasury Tracker takes that document
and draws it in a way anyone can

00:00:49.267 --> 00:00:52.587
read. The whole budget becomes one shape,

00:00:53.327 --> 00:00:55.927
and every department is a slice of it,

00:00:55.927 --> 00:00:58.367
sized by what it actually receives.

00:00:59.209 --> 00:01:01.360
Which means before you
have read a single number,

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:05.080
you already have the answer to
the first question anybody asks.

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:08.000
What is this government mostly doing?

00:01:08.671 --> 00:01:10.849
Then you press a slice and it opens.

00:01:11.409 --> 00:01:13.049
The department breaks into its divisions.

00:01:13.309 --> 00:01:14.889
A division breaks into its programs.

00:01:15.509 --> 00:01:17.689
The same picture, one level down.

00:01:18.317 --> 00:01:20.860
You can keep going until
the slices stop dividing,

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:23.940
and the trail at the top
always tells you where in

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:25.040
the budget you are standing.

00:01:25.623 --> 00:01:27.592
At the bottom you reach the line items.

00:01:28.052 --> 00:01:31.372
What was budgeted, and next
to it what was actually spent.

00:01:31.991 --> 00:01:34.661
And where the records
support it, one step further.

00:01:35.261 --> 00:01:39.841
The individual payments. Who
was paid, how much, and when.

00:01:40.547 --> 00:01:42.693
That's the whole trick. It's not new data.

00:01:42.873 --> 00:01:46.053
It's the same data the
government already published,

00:01:46.633 --> 00:01:50.473
arranged so a person can follow it
instead of exploring spreadsheets.

00:01:51.134 --> 00:01:52.914
A budget answers more than one question,

00:01:53.214 --> 00:01:54.674
so the tool keeps them apart.

00:01:55.357 --> 00:01:55.613
Money out: how the money is spent.

00:01:55.613 --> 00:01:56.713
Money in: where it came
from in the first place.

00:01:58.033 --> 00:02:03.873
And for the governments
that publish it, a third:

00:02:05.093 --> 00:02:08.313
what the workforce is paid,
department by department.

00:02:08.986 --> 00:02:11.833
Change the year, and
the whole shape redraws.

00:02:12.457 --> 00:02:14.127
One year tells you what
a government funds.

00:02:14.767 --> 00:02:17.467
Several years tell you how it is evolving.

00:02:18.027 --> 00:02:21.127
What grew, what shrank,
and what quietly went away.

00:02:21.718 --> 00:02:24.162
And your city is not the only
government taking your money.

00:02:24.836 --> 00:02:28.178
You pay a city, a county, a
state and a federal government,

00:02:28.438 --> 00:02:30.358
and each one keeps its own books.

00:02:31.318 --> 00:02:33.978
Treasury Tracker draws
all four the same way,

00:02:34.038 --> 00:02:37.858
so you can step between them without
learning a new chart each time.

00:02:38.529 --> 00:02:40.445
The federal budget
brings its own question.

00:02:41.225 --> 00:02:44.165
Do you want to see what the
money is for, or who spends it?

00:02:44.701 --> 00:02:47.461
Those are two completely different
pictures of the same money.

00:02:47.461 --> 00:02:49.701
By purpose, and by department.

00:02:50.381 --> 00:02:53.141
You can switch between them
and watch the shape change.

00:02:53.865 --> 00:02:57.356
Federal numbers can get pretty large,
so you can switch the whole view out

00:02:57.356 --> 00:02:59.936
of raw dollars and into
dollars per person.

00:03:00.796 --> 00:03:04.496
Trillions mean nothing. Your
share of it means something.

00:03:05.229 --> 00:03:08.823
Which raises the question you should
ask of any chart anybody ever shows

00:03:08.823 --> 00:03:11.725
you. Where did these numbers come from?

00:03:12.281 --> 00:03:14.076
Out of the government's own filings.

00:03:14.816 --> 00:03:17.536
The published budget, the
annual financial report,

00:03:17.996 --> 00:03:19.736
the Treasury's own spending records.

00:03:20.196 --> 00:03:23.483
Every view names its source
and links directly to it.

00:03:24.051 --> 00:03:26.028
Where a description is the
government's own wording,

00:03:26.528 --> 00:03:29.228
it stays their wording, and it says so.

00:03:29.888 --> 00:03:33.448
Where we summarised one from
the line items underneath it,

00:03:33.728 --> 00:03:37.508
that says so too. You can always
get back to the original page.

00:03:38.291 --> 00:03:41.323
On the federal budget the
method is written out in full.

00:03:41.783 --> 00:03:43.743
What is counted and what is not.

00:03:44.243 --> 00:03:47.743
Why the picture can add up to
more than the official total.

00:03:48.003 --> 00:03:49.483
Where a negative amount went.

00:03:49.743 --> 00:03:50.829
How far into the year the data runs.

00:03:51.413 --> 00:03:55.025
That panel exists because a chart
with no method behind it is only a

00:03:55.025 --> 00:03:59.408
confident drawing. If you want to
check our arithmetic, we tell you how.

00:03:59.997 --> 00:04:01.901
Treasury Tracker is in alpha.

00:04:02.554 --> 00:04:04.181
The federal budget is there for anybody.

00:04:04.721 --> 00:04:07.481
Below that it is city by
city, county by county,

00:04:07.801 --> 00:04:11.461
state by state, and every one of
them is a separate piece of work.

00:04:12.161 --> 00:04:15.791
Find the filing. Read
it. Map it. Check it.

00:04:16.389 --> 00:04:18.531
So search for your city.
If it is not in yet,

00:04:18.791 --> 00:04:21.811
the site says so plainly, and
it will not quietly hand you

00:04:21.811 --> 00:04:23.311
somebody else's budget instead.

00:04:24.018 --> 00:04:25.416
It's worth saying what this is for.

00:04:26.072 --> 00:04:29.272
Not to make you angry about
a line item. Angry is easy.

00:04:29.862 --> 00:04:32.742
It's so you can vote with
confidence and context.

00:04:33.562 --> 00:04:36.722
Hearing a number on its own can
be made to mean almost anything.

00:04:37.542 --> 00:04:42.476
That same number inside the whole
picture usually means just one thing.

00:04:43.033 --> 00:04:46.073
If we want a democracy
that is run by the people,

00:04:46.333 --> 00:04:49.533
we need to make it easier
for the people to understand

00:04:49.533 --> 00:04:51.113
where their money is going.

00:04:51.730 --> 00:04:56.260
This one is live today, at treasury
tracker dot empowered dot vote.

00:04:56.905 --> 00:04:58.830
Find your city. Open the budget.

00:04:59.350 --> 00:05:02.150
Follow one slice all the way
down to a name and a date,

00:05:02.150 --> 00:05:05.070
and see how it feels to actually know.

00:05:05.782 --> 00:05:09.397
That is Treasury Tracker.
Go follow your money.
